<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Peace of Montreal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1701</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaver-wars">Beaver Wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> was a peace treaty signed in August 1701 by the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>, Louis-Hector de Callière, and the representatives of about forty Native nations, among them the Iroquois Confederacy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s Native allies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a>. It ended nearly a century of intermittent war, and the Iroquois promised to stay neutral in conflicts between France and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Over 1,300 delegates attended the conference.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-peace-of-montreal.jpg" alt="Great Peace of Montreal" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Secrétaire de Callière et autre main (pour les pictogrammes) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grande_Paix_Montreal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
